#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# seed

#% text
# `seed(n)` resets the session's random generator to the state given by the integer `n`.

#% text
# `seed()` resets the generator to a fresh seed taken from the operating system.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# After `seed(n)`, the same sequence of random draws follows every time, in every session.

#% item
# The seed may be any integer, negative or large.

#% item
# Every random function draws from the one session generator: `random_real`, `random_integer`, `random_normal`, `random_choice`, `random_sample` and `random_variate`.

#% item
# `random_seed()` gives the seed in use, so that `seed(random_seed())` at the start of a run reproduces its draws.

#% item
# The value of `seed(n)` is `null`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The same seed gives the same number:

#% cell
seed(42); random_real()
#% output
#= 0.3354948980859701

#% cell
seed(42); random_real()
#% output
#= 0.3354948980859701

#% text
# A different seed gives different numbers:

#% cell
seed(5); random_integer([1, 100], 3)
#% output
#= [16, 47, 78]

#% cell
seed(6); random_integer([1, 100], 3)
#% output
#= [75, 37, 90]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Negative and large seeds:

#% cell
seed(-3); random_real()
#% output
#= 0.7452519927418313

#% cell
seed(10^30); random_real()
#% output
#= 0.14631505769903186

#% text
# The seed in use:

#% cell
seed(42); random_seed()
#% output
#= 42

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Make a simulation repeatable: the same seed gives the same mean of a thousand draws.

#% cell
fn sample_mean(k) = { seed(k); mean(random_variate(normal_distribution(), 1000)) }

#% cell
[sample_mean(1), sample_mean(1), sample_mean(2)]
#% output
#= [-0.014223777477516185, -0.014223777477516185, 0.021634131600667073]

#% text
# Record a fresh seed so that a run can be repeated later:

#% cell
seed(); r = random_seed(); seed(r); a1 = random_real(); seed(r); a2 = random_real(); a1 == a2
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# One draw of a list gives the same numbers as the same number of single draws:

#% cell
seed(5); [random_real(), random_real()]
#% output
#= [0.15201353064460954, 0.4626817449000663]

#% cell
seed(5); random_real([0, 1], 2)
#% output
#= [0.15201353064460954, 0.4626817449000663]

#% text
# All the random functions share the generator, so `random_normal` and `random_variate` of the standard normal distribution agree:

#% cell
seed(5); random_normal()
#% output
#= -1.8879088883270938

#% cell
seed(5); random_variate(normal_distribution())
#% output
#= -1.8879088883270938

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The seed must be an integer:

#% cell
seed(2.5)
#% output
#! failure(argument, seed, "the seed must be an integer")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `random_seed`, `random_real`, `random_integer`, `random_normal`, `random_variate`
